{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read ~/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/spec-start/SKILL.md ~/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/SKILL.md ~/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/grilling/SKILL.md'", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read ~/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/spec-start/SKILL.md ~/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/docs-conventions/SKILL.md ~/.codex/plugins/cache/yousefallaban/scrubjay/0.1.0/skills/grilling/SKILL.md'", "aggregated_output": "---\nname: spec-start\ndescription: Start a spec from a sentence, a ticket or commits already made. Creates the spec folder and the branch, sets the kind, feature or fix, settles why and what by grilling for a feature or in one pass for a fix, writes the claims under Add, Change and Remove, offers to map a domain that has no behavior docs, and records the owner's go only when the user reports one. Use when a user describes something to build, change or fix, asks to start or write a spec, runs spec-start, or reports that the owner approved a spec, so the go gets recorded.\n---\n\n# spec-start\n\nFrom an intent to a spec the owner can say go to, with nothing left open that only the owner can answer.\n\n## Process\n\n1. Load docs-conventions and grilling. `references/spec.md` and the claim format apply.\n2. Read what the spec starts from: a sentence, a ticket key with a sentence, or the commits on the current branch when the user says the work is already done. Derive a kebab-case slug of two to five words and the id `<today>-<slug>`. Record the start as `request:`, and the ticket key as `issue:` when there is one.\n3. Set the kind. A `feature` adds or changes behavior and gets grilled, planned and needs the owner's go before planning. A `fix` is a bug, a small change, a chore or a refactor: one pass, a failing test first when it changes behavior, and the owner's go before ship only when it has claims. When unsure, ask with a recommendation.\n4. Create the branch named after the slug from the main branch, unless the branch already exists or the user is already on it. Create `<docs-path>/specs/<id>/spec.md` from the template.\n5. Find the domain. Read `index.md`. If the domain has no row, ask who owns it and what it covers, and add the row now, before any go is asked. If the spec touches a domain with no `behavior/<domain>/` folder, offer to map it first: \"Map <domain> first?\" and run docs-map when the user says yes.\n6. Settle why and what. For a feature, grill in rounds until nothing is left that only the owner can answer, writing the why and each claim into `spec.md` as it settles. For a fix, write the spec in one pass from the request and the code, and ask only about what is ambiguous. Why is 2 to 5 lines, the problem and its cost, no solution.\n7. Write What changes as claims under `### Add · <domain>/<capability>`, `### Change · ...` and `### Remove · ...`, only the headings that apply. A Change carries `was:` with the current text from `behavior/`. A Remove carries `reason:`. A fix with no behavior change writes \"No behavior change.\" So does a fix for code that breaks a claim `behavior/` already states: the claim doesn't change, the code catches up with it, so write \"No behavior change.\" and name the claim the fix restores, as `restores: <domain>/<capability>#<id>`, so the failing test carries that reference. For a feature, write Not doing. Questions only the owner can answer and that the session didn't settle go under Open questions with a recommended answer each.\n8. The go. The owner is the one `index.md` lists for the domain. Record `approved: {by: <owner>, via: <in session | link | PR>}` only when the user reports a go, and only when Open questions is empty. When the user is the owner, their \"go\" in the session is the go, recorded as `via: in session`. Never infer a go from enthusiasm, silence or \"looks good\".\n9. Report: the spec path, the branch, the kind, and the next step, spec-plan for a feature with its go, spec-build for a fix.\n\n## Verification\n\n- The spec folder and the branch exist, the branch name is the slug, and `spec.md` has `request:` and, when given, `issue:`.\n- `kind` is feature or fix, and a feature has Not doing.\n- Every claim has an ID and an `e.g.` line, every Change has `was:`, every Remove has `reason:`.\n- `approved` is present only if the user reported a go and Open questions is gone.\n- No `<!--` guidance left in `spec.md`.\n\n## Rationalizations and red flags\n\n| Excuse | Rule |\n| --- | --- |\n| \"The request is clear enough, I'll skip the rounds.\" | A feature is grilled. If the first round has nothing to ask, say so and show why. |\n| \"They sounded happy, that counts as the go.\" | A go is reported, never inferred. |\n| \"One open question is fine, I'll record the go anyway.\" | Open questions must be empty first. |\n| \"I'll describe how to build it in the spec, it helps.\" | The spec holds why and what. How goes to plan.md. |\n| \"No behavior file for this domain, I'll write claims from memory.\" | Offer docs-map first, or look up the code before writing a `was:`. |\n\n## Fallbacks\n\n- No question tool: grill in plain text, numbered, with recommended answers, and wait.\n- No git: create the folder and tell the user which branch to create.\n---\nname: docs-conventions\ndescription: The file charter and the claim format for a project's memory in docs/. Load before writing or changing any file under docs/, the index, glossary, behavior, architecture, decisions, specs, plans, tasks or reference files, and before editing the project-memory block in AGENTS.md. It says which file holds what, what each never contains, where misplaced content goes, and how a claim is written.\nuser-invocable: false\n---\n\n# Docs conventions\n\nProject memory lives under the docs path the project-memory block in AGENTS.md names, `docs/` unless the block says otherwise. Every file has one job. Content that matches a file's \"never contains\" column goes to the file the charter names for it.\n\n## Process\n\n1. Find the file's row in the charter. Read its reference under `references/` for the sections and their rules, and start from its template under `assets/`.\n2. Write only what the file's job covers. Move anything else to the file the row names.\n3. Remove every `<!-- -->` guidance comment. Delete an optional section that would be empty. Never write \"None\" or \"TBD\". Keep heading names and their order exactly as the template has them.\n4. Keep the frontmatter keys the template has, with no comments in the frontmatter. A quoted slot such as `id: \"{{slug}}\"` is filled without the quotes, unless the value needs them to stay valid YAML.\n5. Write claims in the claim format below, 3 to 7 per capability.\n\n## The charter\n\n| File | Job | Contains | Never contains, and where that goes | Template | Reference |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `index.md` | map of the product and its docs | what it is, the domains with their owners, where things are | details of any domain: `behavior/` | `assets/index.md` | `references/index.md` |\n| `glossary.md` | shared words | term, meaning, name in code, words to avoid | rules or behavior: `behavior/` | none, one line per term | `references/glossary.md` |\n| `behavior/<domain>/<capability>.md` | what the product does now | claims with IDs, one example each | how it's built: `architecture/`; history: git; plans: `specs/` | `assets/behavior-capability.md` | `references/behavior.md` |\n| `architecture/<area>.md` | how it's built now | one area per file, created when it first has content | behavior: `behavior/`; history: git; plans: `specs/` | `assets/architecture/` | `references/architecture.md` |\n| `decisions/<date>-<slug>.md` | why a lasting choice was made | context, options, decision, consequences | step-by-step detail: `plan.md` or `reference/` | `assets/decision.md` | `references/decision.md` |\n| `specs/<id>/spec.md` | why this change, and exactly what changes | why, claims under Add, Change and Remove, not doing, open questions | how it's built: `plan.md`; progress: `tasks.md` | `assets/spec.md` | `references/spec.md` |\n| `specs/<id>/plan.md` | how this change is built | approach, files to touch, architecture to update at ship, risks | the why: `spec.md`; progress: `tasks.md` | `assets/plan.md` | `references/plan.md` |\n| `specs/<id>/tasks.md` | where the work stands | session lines, tasks with the claims that prove them, short notes | design discussion: `plan.md` or `decisions/` | `assets/tasks.md` | `references/tasks.md` |\n| `specs/<id>/reference/*` | working material for this spec | anything the spec needs, opening with `Purpose: ...` | the why, the plan, progress: the three files above | none | `references/reference.md` |\n| AGENTS.md block | the workflow and where memory lives | the managed block between the markers | project knowledge: `docs/` | `assets/agents-block.md` | `references/agents-block.md` |\n\n## The claim format\n\n```\n- <Actor> <does or sees something observable, present tense> {#claim-id}\n  e.g. <a concrete situation> -> <the observable result>\n```\n\nOne testable sentence per claim, then one concrete example. IDs are kebab-case and unique within their capability. The full reference is `domain/capability#claim-id`; tests carry it in their names, and tasks use it whenever the bare ID is not unique in the spec. Observable behavior only: \"keeps one entry\", never \"uses a unique index\". A claim in `spec.md` reads exactly as it will in `behavior/`, so shipping copies it without rewording.\n\n## Versions\n\n`index.md` carries `docs-format`. A template change applies to new files only. Shipped specs and existing files are never rewritten to a newer format.\n\n## Verification\n\n- The file has the template's frontmatter keys and headings, in the template's order, and no `<!-- -->` comment anywhere.\n- No section is empty, and \"None\" and \"TBD\" appear nowhere.\n- Every claim has an ID and an `e.g.` line, and a capability has 3 to 7 of them.\n- Nothing in the file matches its \"never contains\" column.\n---\nname: grilling\ndescription: How to settle intent before any code. Question rounds where every question is answerable now and comes with a recommended answer, facts looked up in the code and docs instead of asked, and each answer written where it belongs the moment it settles. Loaded by spec-start, spec-plan and docs-map whenever something only the owner can answer is still open.\nuser-invocable: false\n---\n\n# Grilling\n\nSettle what only the owner can settle, in rounds, and write each answer down as soon as it lands.\n\n## Process\n\n1. Before asking anything, look it up. The docs path's `index.md`, `glossary.md`, `behavior/`, `architecture/` and `decisions/`, then the code and its tests. A question the code or the docs can answer is never put to the owner; the answer is used and, when it matters, quoted with its source.\n2. Ask in numbered rounds of 3 to 5 questions. Every question is answerable now, by the owner, from what they know. Each has the form: the question, one line on why it matters or what changes with a different answer, and `Recommended: <answer>`. The rounds move from the goal and who it is for, to scope and boundaries, to edge cases and data, to what is deliberately out.\n3. Write each answer where its topic belongs the moment it settles, before the next round: the why and the what into `spec.md`, as claims in the claim format; the how into `plan.md` or a `reference/` file; a lasting choice into a decision record; a settled term into `glossary.md`, sorted. Nothing waits for the end of the interview.\n4. Stop when nothing is left that only the owner can answer. What remains answerable from the code is looked up, not asked. When the owner is not in the session and questions remain, they go under Open questions in `spec.md`, each with its recommended answer, and the spec stays a draft until the owner answers.\n5. Never ask filler, never ask twice, and never ask what the owner already said. A recommended answer is a real recommendation, not a hedge.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Every question in the transcript carries `Recommended:`.\n- No question was answerable from the docs or the code.\n- Every settled answer is in a file before the round after it.\n- The interview ended with Open questions empty, or with each remaining question being one only the owner can answer.\n\n## Rationalizations and red flags\n\n| Excuse | Rule |\n| --- | --- |\n| \"The answer is obvious, I'll assume it.\" | If only the owner can answer it, ask it, with the obvious answer as the recommendation. |\n| \"I'll ask everything in one big round to save time.\" | 3 to 5 per round. A wall of questions gets skimmed. |\n| \"Let me ask which database they use.\" | That is in `architecture/stack.md` or the code. Look it up. |\n| \"I'll write it all down at the end.\" | Each answer is written when it settles. A session can end at any moment. |\n| \"They said 'just build it', so no questions.\" | Open questions that only the owner can answer stay open, and the spec stays a draft. |\n\n## Fallbacks\n\n- No question tool: ask in plain text, numbered, with the recommended answers, and wait.\n- No owner in the session: write the remaining questions under Open questions and stop.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
