{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/zsh -lc 'rtk read docs/behavior/returns/request-return.md docs/behavior/returns/calculate-refund.md docs/behavior/returns/process-return.md'", "aggregated_output": "---\ncapability: returns/request-return\n---\n# Request a return\n\n- Customers can request a return only for an order marked delivered with a recorded delivery time. {#require-delivery}\n  e.g. An order is still paid -> requesting a return fails with \"Only delivered orders can be returned\".\n\n- Customers can submit a return exactly 30 days after delivery, but receive a rejection once more than 30 days have elapsed. {#enforce-return-window}\n  e.g. An order was delivered at 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z -> a return at 2026-01-31T00:00:00Z is accepted, while one a millisecond later is rejected.\n\n- Customers must include at least one item in a return request. {#require-items}\n  e.g. A delivered order is within the return window, but the request has no items -> it fails with \"A return needs at least one item\".\n\n- Customers receive a rejection for a return entry whose product is absent from the order. {#require-ordered-product}\n  e.g. An order contains only wool socks, and the customer requests a headlamp return -> the request fails because the headlamp is not on the order.\n\n- Customers must specify a whole-number quantity of at least one for each return entry. {#require-positive-whole-quantity}\n  e.g. A customer requests a return of 1.5 wool socks -> the request fails with \"Quantity must be a whole number of at least 1\".\n\n- Customers receive a rejection when a single return entry exceeds the quantity on the order's first matching line for that product. {#limit-each-entry-quantity}\n  e.g. An order has one line containing one pair of wool socks, and a return entry requests two -> the request fails with \"Cannot return more wool-socks than were ordered\".\n\n- Customers receive a new return in requested status for an accepted submission. {#start-requested}\n  e.g. An order containing one pair of wool socks was delivered on January 1, 2026, and the customer submits a valid return on January 20 -> the new return has status requested.\n---\ncapability: returns/calculate-refund\n---\n# Calculate a refund\n\n- Customers receive a refund calculation for the submitted quantities using the unit prices recorded on the order. {#value-returned-items}\n  e.g. An undiscounted order contains two rain jackets at 1990 cents each and one trail map at 850 cents -> returning one jacket produces a refund amount of 1990 cents.\n\n- Customers receive a refund calculation reduced by the order's discount percentage. {#apply-order-discount}\n  e.g. A water bottle has an order price of 2000 cents and the order has a 10 percent discount -> returning the bottle produces a refund amount of 1800 cents.\n\n- Customers receive one refund amount rounded to whole cents after the discount is applied to the combined value of the returned entries. {#round-combined-refund}\n  e.g. Two rain jackets cost 1990 cents each on an order with a 15 percent discount -> returning both produces a refund amount of 3383 cents.\n---\ncapability: returns/process-return\n---\n# Approve and record a refund\n\n- Support can approve a requested return, changing its status to approved. {#approve-requested-return}\n  e.g. A wool-socks return has status requested -> approving it produces a return with status approved.\n\n- Support receives a rejection when approving a return that is already approved or refunded. {#reject-invalid-approval}\n  e.g. A return already has status approved -> another approval fails with \"A approved return cannot be approved\".\n\n- Support can mark an approved return as refunded. {#record-approved-refund}\n  e.g. A wool-socks return has status approved -> recording its refund produces a return with status refunded.\n\n- Support receives a rejection when recording a refund for a return that is requested or already refunded. {#require-approved-refund}\n  e.g. A return still has status requested -> recording its refund fails with \"Only an approved return can be refunded\".\n\n- Support retains the calculated refund amount when approving a return and marking it refunded. {#preserve-refund-amount}\n  e.g. A requested wool-socks return has a refund amount of 1200 cents -> the approved and refunded results also record 1200 cents.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
